WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.240
It was 15 years ago, almost to the day, April 
27th, 2011. I had just opened my very first  

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:10.400
real business. It was an iPhone repair store in 
Tuscaloosa, Alabama. And it was right next to a  

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.600
subway. It smelled like fresh bread all day 
long, and I loved it. But after a few weeks,  

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.320
nobody was coming in the store. The haters 
were right. I was going to fail. I had signed  

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.160
a personal guarantee on this 5-year lease and a 
newborn son at home who is now almost 16. And yes,  

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.760
you may have heard the story before, but stick 
with me for one minute because this is a brand  

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.560
new, fresh episode. And then I was lying in bed 
on a Sunday night staring at the ceiling, and I  

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.520
had a stupid idea. Now, keep in mind, I don't 
drink. I'd never been to a bar in Tuscaloosa,  

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.840
Alabama in my life. That's where I was living. 
But I thought, hey, college students go to bars,  

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.800
they get wristbands. What if I put my ad for 
my iPhone repair store on a wristband? So,  

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:54.320
I pulled out my clunky Dell laptop. I Googled 
custom wristbands. They were 2 cents each. I  

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.840
ordered a few boxes and I started distributing 
them to bars around campus. What would happen?  

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:03.600
Well, college students would go out, get drunk, 
break their iPhone, wake up the next morning,  

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.160
hung over with a wristband on their wrist that 
said phone restore, and then they would walk into  

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:14.080
my shop holding a broken phone and a ratty, 
smelly wristband. It changed everything. My  

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.240
store survived. We opened three more stores, 
and eventually we sold the business for about  

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.880
a million bucks. And here's the thing that 
nobody tells you about running a business.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.440
On the surface, the wristband was like a 
marketing gimmick, but that's not what it  

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.480
was. It was communication. The kid was going 
to break his phone either way. The question  

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:40.720
was whether my message was the one that his brain 
would grab when he woke up the next morning. That  

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:47.120
is communication. That's the game here. Remember, 
I've started about 80 businesses in the last 17,  

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:52.080
18 years, and I've recorded almost 300 
episodes of this podcast. I've watched  

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:56.800
founders close deals worth tens of millions 
on this exact microphone. I've watched other  

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.760
business owners fumble in real time. So, I want to 
talk about seven different communication tactics  

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.880
that the best of the best do that everyone else 
misses out on. These are backed by real research  

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.200
and they're backed by my own stories of when 
they worked for me and also a couple stories  

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.080
of when I bombed because I didn't implement these 
tactics. So, if you're a founder, a salesperson,  

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.360
a wannabe founder, entrepreneur, operator, 
an employee asking for a raise, anything,  

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.200
this episode is for you because the money is found 
in improving your communication skills. And these  

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.440
principles apply no matter who or what you are. 
So, I'm going to end this with a wild story,  

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:36.320
but I want to start by going through these 
seven tactics. Number one, the hook, the first  

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.120
sentence. That's everything. The first sentence 
is worth more than everything after it combined.  

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.800
Open your inbox right now and look at all of the 
cold pitches you get and delete in 3 seconds. Hi,  

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.640
my name is I'm reaching out because would love to 
hop on a quick call. No, stop. You're just going  

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.800
to delete those. Everyone deletes those. Yet, 
you're also sending those. And around that same  

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.760
time as my wristband story, I learned the hard way 
that the CMO, the chief marketing officer of Best  

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:06.560
Buy, deletes those as well. These people have 
heard that line 10,000 times and they're going  

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.120
to tune you out before sentence two. You're 
sounding like a car alarm in a parking lot.  

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.080
It gets shut out after a while. Now, the cold 
pitches that I hear or read that stand out,  

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:21.840
they hit me with something specific to me. Not 
AI generated specific. Hey, Chris, I see you  

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.880
went to the University of Alabama and that you 
currently run it. No, not specific like that.  

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.680
Specific to a tweet that I posted yesterday or 
an episode that I aired just last week. Honestly,  

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.840
if it's AI generated, doesn't even matter because 
it's still relevant to me. This is something that  

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.880
proves the person actually paid attention. A few 
months ago, I got a cold email that started with  

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.120
this one sentence. Hey, you said on a recent 
episode that your favorite type of business is  

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.600
one a high schooler could run. Well, I built 
one. Here's what it looks like. I read that  

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:55.280
entire email and I responded within 30 minutes and 
he's coming on the show now. Many, many moons ago,  

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:00.560
I cold called the chief marketing officer of Best 
Buy to get my iPhone repair company into their  

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:04.480
stores. I thought, "Hey, they have Geek Squad. 
They're fixing computers. They need to fix phones,  

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.840
too. This is a no-brainer offer. And I also did 
reverse engineering research, aka Googling, and  

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.760
learned that Geek Squad was a very small business 
at the time they were purchased. They bought a  

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.480
system and a process. And really, that's all I 
had at the time. And so, I thought, okay, I don't  

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.520
need to be huge for them to buy me. But before I 
made this call, I met with maybe the only mentor  

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.840
I've ever had in my life. His name's John Nolan. 
Love the guy. And he asked me about this woman,  

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.240
the CMO at the time. We pulled up her LinkedIn 
and he told me a fact about her. He scrolled way  

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.760
down to the bottom of the LinkedIn. That's where 
the magic is. He saw that she had played women's  

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.880
basketball at Gonzaga and he said, "Chris, that 
one fact tells you everything you need to know  

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.680
about her personality type. It also tells you how 
to open the conversation. So that's what I did  

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:51.200
when I called her. I led with the Gonzaga thing 
before I got to me and my pitch. I led with her  

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:55.600
because what do people care about? Themselves." 
That first sentence is going to do 80% of the  

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.240
leg work. Everything after the first sentence is 
just confirming what the first sentence already  

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.320
promised. So, if the opener is generic, they're 
going to assume that everything else is generic,  

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:10.480
and they won't even read it. So, tactic number 
two, admit a flaw fast. I know this one's going to  

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.920
feel wrong instinctually to you. Trust me, there's 
a thing in social psychology called the Prattfall  

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.880
effect. And that's when you admit a flaw or a 
weakness and then people somehow, for some reason,  

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.880
trust you more. That polish and sheen that 
you're always trying to convey to others,  

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.440
you think it makes you look more credible when 
actually it's having the opposite effect. So,  

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.000
here's an example from my own life. When I first 
opened my first iPhone repair store, I had never  

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.000
fixed an iPhone in my life. People would walk 
in with a broken iPhone, and I would say, "Okay,  

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.880
cool, cool, cool. Yeah, okay. Uh-huh. Oh, okay. 
Yeah, give us a few hours. We'll figure it out."  

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.200
Then I would go in the back, pull up YouTube, and 
learn how to fix their iPhone with their phone  

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.600
in my hand in real time. Believe it or not, just 
because you're watching this doesn't mean you're  

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.280
subscribed to my channel. YouTube's going to 
show you stuff even if you're not subscribed to  

00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:00.000
it. Over half of people that watch my videos are 
not subscribed. It would mean the world to me if  

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.560
you just hit subscribe. Thank you so much. Now, 
maybe I should have been faking confidence that  

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.880
I didn't actually have. But instead, I started 
practicing what happened when I told customers  

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:13.280
the truth. Yeah, I can figure out how to fix this. 
It disarmed them. And any concern that they might  

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:19.280
have had about my credibility or lack thereof was 
offset by the frankness of it all. They'd laugh,  

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.440
they'd come back, they'd tell their friends, 
and they became regulars. Believe it or not,  

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.440
there are more regulars in the iPhone repair 
business than you'd ever believe. But remember,  

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:32.320
if I would have been polished, I probably would 
have lost those customers. But the honest version  

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:36.800
got them. And I see this on my show every week. 
I don't know if you heard this episode from about  

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:41.840
a month ago. Mike Yodar had that drone deer 
recovery business turned drone agriculture  

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.560
business. He made 50 grand in his first 10 weeks 
doing agricultural spring. But after we stopped  

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:50.960
recording, he just mentioned offhand that he grew 
up Amish. And I'm like, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.  

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.600
That's the story. can we turn the camera back on? 
He said yes. We talked for 20 more minutes about  

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.720
what it was like to leave his community. And that 
little clip outperformed all the business stuff.  

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.720
And this is a business podcast. We'll link to that 
episode in the description, by the way. It's one  

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:10.000
of my favorites of all time. But it's a disarming 
story and it shows the person behind the story and  

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:15.120
people love that. So when you walk into a pitch, 
lead with one specific thing that you tried that  

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.360
did not work. Here's what we got wrong in year 
one. Well, humility. Who leads with humility  

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:23.520
nowadays? Just watch. when you say something like 
that, how the room will shift towards you. And  

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.920
I say room, it doesn't need to be literal. This 
could be on Zoom, over an email, text, whatever.  

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:33.280
The cleaned up version of yourself sells less, 
but the honest version sells more. Which I've  

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:37.280
said this a million times, honesty is profitable. 
Doing the right thing is profitable. Being a good  

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.640
human is more profitable in the long run than 
otherwise. All right, tactic number three. Stories  

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:48.240
sell. Sell your story. The data confirms this. The 
episodes of mine that crush on the show are never  

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.960
the ones where a guest reads off their profit and 
loss statement. It's the ones with one specific  

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:57.200
moment that a founder can't shake. Also, anytime 
I reference an old episode in this, we're going to  

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:01.120
link to it, so just check it in the description. 
I interviewed a woman named Shannon. She has a  

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:06.240
roadside peach stand business, and she gave me 
all the numbers, the margins, cost, the logistics,  

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:12.000
etc. But the clip that resonated the most was when 
she talked about pulling up to a gas station in  

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.920
South Carolina at 5:00 a.m. with her 11-year-old 
son in the passenger seat and how she learned how  

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.240
to run a peach stand from her mentor. That's 
the thing that people talk about. So, anytime  

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:27.440
you're pitching anything, leave the data and the 
numbers in the deck, but the story should be the  

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:33.360
thing that you tell yourself because investors, 
customers, vendors, business partners, anyone,  

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:37.760
they're buying the founder. The numbers are just 
the receipts. I'm sure you've heard on Shark Tank  

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:41.440
people say, "Oh, I bet on the jockey, not the 
horse." Well, that's true. Not just in investing,  

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.680
but in life, in business, in life, and anything. 
It's about people. It all comes down to people.  

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:51.920
So, if your pitch is 90% slides and 10% story, 
flip it and watch your close rate skyrocket. Okay.  

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:56.640
Number four, the dumber it sounds, the smarter 
you look. Let me tell you about a ship. Stockholm,  

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:01.440
Sweden, in the 1600s. The Vasa was the biggest, 
baddest warship that had ever been built. It  

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:07.040
had 64 bronze cannons, two rows of 16 on each 
side. It was the best of the best. Gold paint,  

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:12.080
wood carvings, the pride of the king. They'd never 
seen anything like it. And on its maiden voyage,  

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:17.440
August 10th, 1628, the Vasa sailed out of the 
harbor and it was supposed to do a parade lap so  

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:22.800
everyone could see how awesome it was. But half a 
mile out, a minor breeze caught the sails and the  

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:28.000
ship tipped to one side. Then it tipped back, 
tipped further. The port holes for the cannons  

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.440
were open because they wanted to show off. It 
looked cooler that way. And they filled with  

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.160
water. The ship sank right there in the harbor 
in front of everyone. 30 people tragically died  

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:43.280
and 333 years later divers pulled up the ship and 
now it's in a museum right next to where it sank  

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.400
originally and I saw it in person in 2018 and it 
was amazing. It was the best museum I've ever been  

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:54.080
to and I realized that the Vasa sank because the 
builders were warned that it might be topheavy but  

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:58.160
they ignored it because it looked awesome and they 
wanted to build something that looked awesome not  

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.480
something that was necessarily safe. Ideally you 
have both but they chose for something that looked  

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:06.880
awesome. They didn't add enough ballast. Now 
ballast is what goes in the bottom of the hole to  

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.520
make it more bottom heavy to weigh it down to keep 
it more stable, but everything above the waterline  

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:16.000
is pretty and that's what they focused on. So the 
vasa is what a bad pitch looks like. It's what  

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:22.240
bad communication looks like. Beautiful slides, 
big words, tam, arr blended CAC, vertical sass,  

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:27.040
reoccurring revenue, motion. It's all pretty above 
the waterline, but hollow below. And the first  

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:32.320
breeze you get, aka the first real piece of push 
back or question you get, the whole thing will  

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:38.000
tip over. And research at the University of Munich 
backs this up. They noticed that speakers who use  

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:44.400
simple, clear language, get rated as more smart 
and more competent than otherwise. Speakers who  

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:50.480
lean into jargon, big words trying to sound smart, 
sound dumb. They get rated lower. And the reason  

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:57.600
is because of what jargon does to a listener's 
brain. It triggers like a quiet alarm that says,  

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:02.560
"I think this guy's hiding something." And I see 
this on my show every week. You might not see it  

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:06.880
as much because we edit it out, but often times 
a founder comes on and I ask what they do and  

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:10.800
they're like, "Oh, I have this vertical SAS 
reoccurring revenue yet." And I'm just like,  

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:16.320
"Bro, stop." Like, do you even know your business? 
The best business owners explain it like a sixth  

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:21.360
grader. What did Phone Restore do? We fixed broken 
iPhones. Four words. Do you remember my Toasted  

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:26.560
Tours episode? What did Kevin do? They put windows 
in shipping containers, put it on a semi-truck,  

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:30.640
and drive people through wine country. Peach 
stands. I sell peaches on the side of the road.  

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:34.720
If you can't explain your business in one short 
sentence, then you don't understand your business  

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:38.720
well enough yet. It's hard to hear, but it's 
true. The first sentence of your pitch should  

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:44.240
be one that your grandmother could repeat to her 
friends. Number five, specifics beat adjectives  

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:48.800
every single time. This is the one that almost 
nobody teaches, and it's the cleanest tell of an  

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:53.200
amateur communicator. Listen to this sentence. We 
saw really strong growth in our first year and we  

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:57.760
believe we can scale rapidly with the right 
investment. Look, strong growth. That's so  

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:03.440
broad. Scale rapidly. How rapidly? What is scale? 
How much growth does that represent? The right  

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:08.400
investment. Is that a million? A trillion dollars? 
Let's deliver the same message with this sentence.  

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:16.400
We grew from 3 to 247 customers. Between December 
and January, we grew from 3 to 247 customers.  

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:22.800
We add 30 new customers a month with no paid 
marketing. We need $400,000 to hire two engineers  

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:27.760
so we can double our growth. Basically the same 
message but a different universe of meaning. Speak  

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:33.200
in numbers. You want to communicate with real 
numbers and proper nouns. That's the whole culture  

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:38.400
of this podcast is being tactical and specific 
and simple and approachable and accessible,  

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:43.520
etc. You know the drill. Name the dollar 
amount and the time frame, the city, the year,  

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:48.640
the marketing method you used. What did Mike Yoder 
say? The drone business. I made 50 grand in my  

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:55.440
first 10 weeks. Connor Gross said, "I wholesaliled 
one self- storage deal for $360,000 of profit."  

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:59.520
Shannon said, "I opened a hundred peach stands 
my first summer." You've heard me talk about  

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:05.440
the business I bought for $750,000 about 5 years 
ago. We made $10 million in 90 days. We didn't  

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:10.560
raise any money. We didn't use any paid marketing. 
That's my story. I could have said we grew really  

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:16.240
fast, but that doesn't tell the story. That's 
not interesting. So strip out every really, very,  

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:21.920
truly, incredibly out of your pitch altogether 
and replace each one with a specific number,  

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:27.600
date, or a name, and your pitch will land twice as 
hard with half as many words. Number six, you want  

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:32.160
to talk in chunks. Force the silence. Here's 
one of my favorite quotes about good writing,  

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:36.800
and I would apply it to good speaking as well. 
This sentence has five words. Here are five more  

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:42.400
words. Fiveword sentences are fine, but several 
together become monotonous. Listen to what is  

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:46.960
happening. The writing is getting boring. The 
sound of it drones. It's like a stuck record.  

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:52.720
The ear demands some variety. Now listen. I vary 
the sentence length and I create music. Music. The  

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:59.040
writing sings. It has a pleasant rhythm, a lil, a 
harmony. I use short sentences and I use sentences  

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:03.600
of medium length. And sometimes when I am certain 
the reader is rested, I will engage him with a  

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:09.280
sentence of considerable length. A sentence that 
burns with energy and builds with all the impetus  

00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:15.120
of a crescendo, the roll of the drums, the crash 
of the symbols, sounds that say, "Listen to this."  

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:21.760
It is important. Good thinking is good writing, is 
good speaking, is good communicating. Okay? Mix up  

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:27.520
your words, mix up your sentences, mix up your 
sentence lengths. The best communicators talk in  

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:33.520
short bursts, but the ramblers, and there's a lot 
of ramblers out there, it just sags. It just loses  

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:38.640
interest. even if the story itself is awesome. 
Now, when people get nervous, the default move  

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:43.040
is just to keep talking. You want to fill the air. 
You want to keep the room warm. So, you just keep  

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:48.480
rambling. You add caveats. You hedge. But don't 
try not to have some self-awareness about that. If  

00:14:48.480 --> 00:14:53.600
you watch a CEO talk the top of their game, think 
of Steve Jobs presenting the new iPhone. They  

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:58.960
land a sentence and they stop, pause, but they 
also know when to not stop, when to keep going,  

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:04.400
when to power through, speed up, or when to 
slow down. And of course, in any negotiation,  

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:08.880
the person doing most of the talking is usually 
the one losing the negotiation. I learned this  

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:14.480
the hard way. It was October 21st, 2021. I was 
sitting in Texas Children's Hospital next to my  

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:19.120
daughter who was waiting on a lung transplant. 
And our biggest customer was 90% of our revenue.  

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:24.000
He was an absolute nightmare, wielding his 
revenue over me like a club. And that afternoon,  

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:28.480
I sat in the hospital and wrote him an email. 
Short paragraphs, no softeners, and this was the  

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:33.360
closing line. Your continued involvement in this 
working relationship is a greater liability to our  

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:38.240
business than the current threats you are making. 
It removed all power from him. One sentence. Put  

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:43.120
your strongest line at the end. Put your strongest 
two lines. No matter what you're communicating,  

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:48.000
if you only want them to remember two sentences. 
You want to put one at the beginning and one  

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:51.920
at the end. What happened in this case? He 
kept talking, kept threatening. Eventually,  

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:56.560
his own team had to step in around him because 
he was a liability. And we never worked with him  

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:00.800
again. And it was the right call. It was the 
only call. So if someone's steamrolling you,  

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:06.160
getting louder will lose you the battle. Going 
longer will lose you the battle. So go shorter.  

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:10.480
There's a quote I read. I can't remember who said 
it, but it was, "I have made this letter longer  

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:15.920
than usual because I have not had the time to make 
it shorter. It's a lot of work to be succinct. It  

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:20.960
takes practice, but it's worth working at." Guys, 
tkowners.com. That's my community where people are  

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:26.080
building businesses. I do AMAs, Q&As's every 
week live. You can ask me anything you want.  

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:29.920
You can have accountability partners. It's about 
a thousand people in there building, starting,  

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:37.440
growing businesses. Check out TKO as in the Kerner 
office, tkowners.com. Number seven, end with a  

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:41.760
recommendation in all of your communications. 
Don't end with a question. People will follow  

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:47.920
a specific recommendation about 60% more often 
than responding to just an open-ended question.  

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:53.280
Because the open-ended question requires them to 
think, exercise their brain. That's friction. How  

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:57.120
many of your outgoing emails end with something 
like, "Let me know what you think." Open to your  

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:01.360
thoughts. Would love to hear your reaction. I do 
that sometimes. I'm guilty of a lot of this stuff.  

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:05.920
But when you end with something like that, then 
it's a signal to the other person that you don't  

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:10.160
fully believe in the thing that you're sending. 
So end it with a recommendation. Something like,  

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:15.440
"Here's what I recommend we do next. We get on a 
20-minute call next Thursday at 2 p.m. and I'll  

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:20.000
walk you through implementation. I'll send the 
calendar invite later tonight." Very specific,  

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:25.920
a recommendation, just one thing. real time on a 
real day. This is what leadership in communication  

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:30.240
looks and sounds like. And people follow leaders. 
They don't follow people that stall and seem  

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:35.200
unsure about what should happen next. Test this 
in your outreach. 50% of your emails end with  

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:39.840
something like, "Let me know what you think." Aka, 
set aside part of your day to think about this and  

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:45.280
then respond to me. Not going to happen. And then 
in the other 50% end with a recommendation. Your  

00:17:45.280 --> 00:17:50.000
response rate will go way up. And yes, all 
of these principles work in person as well.  

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:55.280
end the meeting with here's what we're going 
to do next in a specific date and watch just  

00:17:55.280 --> 00:18:00.000
how quickly things move. It's funny how a lot of 
these principles make sense in retrospect and I  

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.320
look back on my life and I can recognize times 
where I did them well and times where I didn't.  

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:10.080
And usually it was just by accident and I'm only 
able to connect the dots in retrospect. So by no  

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:14.000
means do I want you to think that like I'm this 
master communicator because I'm neglecting to tell  

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:18.400
you about all the times where I roarily screwed 
this up. But one of my favorite stories, my best  

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:23.440
stories of all time is when I pitched to John 
McAfee, the accused murderer, founder of McAfee  

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:27.760
Security. At the time I pitched to him, he was 
hiding out somewhere in Tennessee, but I guessed  

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:33.280
his email address and I wanted to pitch him on 
this software that I was trying to build. And  

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:38.480
he responded. He said, "Come pitch me in person 
on your business." And the business idea, really,  

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:44.080
it was just an idea was this piece of software or 
algorithm or Google sheet really that could help  

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:48.800
predict cryptocurrency price movements. But he 
was so paranoid about his security that he would  

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:55.600
only give me his actual address the morning of the 
meeting. So it was like a late February in 2018,  

00:18:55.600 --> 00:19:00.640
I think. Drove to his house. I pitched him in 
front of an Australian film crew that happened  

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:05.280
to be there anyway to film like an Australian 
version of 60 Minutes about him. And he agreed  

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:10.000
to partner with me for 25% of that business and to 
promote me. And we ended up building a community  

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:16.880
of over 70,000 people. We launched a free airdrop 
token that hit like $30 million in market cap in  

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:21.760
a matter of months. And then, you know, the crypto 
market crashed and everything died. And you know,  

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:26.560
that was a year and a half, two years of my 
life wasted. Not really, but it was a great  

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:31.440
learning experience. And for those months 
that I ran that business with John McAfee,  

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:36.080
he was tweeting about me twice a week. I actually 
tell the full version of the story on episode nine  

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:42.560
of this show if you scroll way back about two 
years to spring of 2024, March of 2024. We link  

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:47.440
that as well. But I'm telling you this story again 
because the entire reason that pitch landed was  

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:51.360
all because of the first sentence that I said 
to him when I first sat down with him. I led  

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:56.960
with a question about something specific he had 
posted online, specifically him. A, it told him  

00:19:56.960 --> 00:20:02.480
I did my research. This was preAI. B, I threw in a 
compliment, and we love compliments. So his brain  

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:08.320
stopped for just a moment. And this was the same 
move that I used on the CMO of Best Buy about,  

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:12.960
you know, Gonzega. And this is the same move that 
I'm teaching you here today. So before you go,  

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:18.560
remember this one thing from all these businesses 
that I've started. Some hit, most failed and or  

00:20:18.560 --> 00:20:23.520
were abandoned. But the ones that hit and the ones 
that failed had the same thing in common. They  

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:28.320
either lived or died on communication. How you 
communicate with your customers, your employees,  

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:32.640
your vendors, your investors, etc. The founders 
I interview on this show that are crushing it  

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:36.640
are not any smarter. They're just better at 
communication. They've done more reps with  

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:41.440
these seven things that I just taught you. That's 
the difference. So, I got to eat my own dog food,  

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:46.640
right? I got to end this with a recommendation. My 
recommendation to you is to pick one move of these  

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:50.640
seven and let me know how it goes in the comments. 
and we'll see you next time on the Kerner

